<header class="page-head"><p class="kicker">Developers</p><h1>The terminal's pages</h1><p class="lead">The small stack language the cable terminal&#x27;s full-screen pages are written in.</p></header>
<nav class="devnav" aria-label="Developer pages"><a href="/dev/">Overview</a> <a href="/dev/building/">Building</a> <a href="/dev/link-protocol/">Link protocol</a> <a href="/dev/update-file/">Update file</a> <a href="/dev/codeplug/">Codeplug format</a> <a href="/dev/terminal-pages/" class=on aria-current=page>Terminal pages</a> <a href="/dev/flash-map.html">SPI flash map</a></nav>
<div class="prose">

<p>The cable terminal's full-screen pages are a program in a small stack language, compiled into an SPI part and run by an interpreter in the firmware (manual TERM-47). What follows is the language's own description, from the program's source.</p>
<p>The terminal's full-screen pages (TERM-11 to TERM-20, TERM-47; decision 346): a small stack language that <code>build.rs</code> compiles into the template pack, the program <code>term::tpl</code> runs from the SPI part. Everything a page shows and does is here: the text, layout, list rows, hints, help, what each key does, the channel list's tools. The firmware keeps the interpreter and the natives listed below (a value, a channel's field, a setting by its menu number, the radio's formatters, a write through the App). The byte format is described in <code>term/tpl.rs</code>.</p>
<p>Words run left to right on a stack of 32-bit numbers. A string "..." prints itself (\" \ \n \xNN; columns counted, clipped at the row's width); <code>raw "..."</code> sends an escape sequence; <code>bold "..."</code> prints in bold; <code>pick "a|b|c"</code> prints piece i (i on the stack). <code>@x</code> pushes variable x, <code>!x</code> stores into it: <code>vars</code> keep their values (16 bits: a zone is -3 for every channel, -1 for none), <code>locals</code> last one run. <code>: name ... ;</code> defines a word (in the lib before the first page, else in that page; <code>( ... )</code> is a comment). <code>if ... else ... then</code>, <code>begin ... while ... repeat</code>, <code>begin ... until</code>, <code>exit</code>. Numbers (16-bit literals): 12, -1, 0x1F, 'c'; <code>const</code> names one. <code>.</code> prints a number, <code>.r</code> / <code>.z</code> (n width) padded with spaces / zeros; <code>at</code> (col) moves right with spaces. <code>\</code> alone starts a comment; a line starting with # is one.</p>
<p>A page: <code>hints "Key=What|..."</code>, <code>help "line\nline"</code>, rules for its body rows (body row 0 is the line under the top line; the first rule that takes a row draws it, run with <code>@i</code> the row in a list):</p>
<pre><code>row N CODE         that row
bot K CODE         K rows above the hints line's row (body - K, body = rows - 2)
list N COUNT CODE  rows N.. for COUNT's count
tail K CODE        K rows after the last list's end
pre CODE           runs before the rules (exit: a blank row)
</code></pre>
<p>and its keys: <code>key KEY CODE</code> (KEY up down left right pgup pgdn home end enter esc line lesc digit, or "c"; letters come lower case; a digit's CODE gets the key on the stack), <code>key pre CODE</code> first (the key on the stack), <code>key else CODE</code> for any other. <code>: prep ... ;</code> runs before each redraw, <code>: poll ... ;</code> each poll while @busy isn't 0. A page is read from the SPI flash with the lib, through a small window each (tpl::Code); a key or a poll that sets <code>!chain</code> to another page runs that page's key or poll next (the channel list's tools are one). The pages' order is term::Page's (a page only chained to comes after them).</p>
